# Check actual implementation behavior

## Capture and check

- Install `pysnappea`; import `snappea`.
- Emit after meaningful committed changes, with relevant IDs, values, and versions.
- Partition by independent history; operations sharing an invariant must reach the same monitor instance.
- Save the observation test with implementation tests so later changes rerun it.

```python
from snappea import check_spec
from storage import ReservationStore


def test_competing_reservations():
    with check_spec(
        "Capacity",
        spec_files=["Reservation.p"],
        strict=True,
        check_hot=True,
        unknown_events="error",
    ):
        store = ReservationStore(1)  # emits eOpened
        first = store.read()
        second = store.read()
        assert store.reserve_if_unchanged("order-a", 1, first)
        assert not store.reserve_if_unchanged("order-b", 1, second)
```

- `check_spec` captures application `emit` calls; it does not intercept arbitrary methods. `check_events(events, "Capacity", spec_files=[...])` checks an existing recording.
- Without application emission, wrap real calls and record actual inputs, results, and public reads. Do not invent internal commits. Hand-written traces test only the monitor.
- Preserve distinctions: mapping all noninitial heads to one value can hide wrong results.
- See [reservation](../examples/reservation/Reservation.p) for saved tests, a rejected defective implementation, and an independent Python invariant.

## Evidence limits

- `strict=True` fails when a selected spec receives no relevant events; it cannot prove every operation emitted.
- `unknown_events="error"` rejects undeclared/misspelled names. CLI observation defaults to ignoring unknown events; declared events ignored by a spec are unchecked by it.
- `check_hot=True` checks unfinished monitor obligations at history end; observation does not explore application schedules.
- Emission before commit does not prove commit. Post-commit emission can be lost on crash.
- Concurrent post-commit emitters can reorder records. Order-sensitive checks need a version/sequence captured at the atomic operation or a controlled test boundary.
- Join child processes before checking their recordings; isolate each test/history. Event `src` identifies its emitting call site in failures.
- Runtime emitters must remain best-effort: no raised errors or blocked requests. Operational evidence should expose drops; explicit tests should validate recordings and fail on violations.

## Maintain correspondence

- Compare real reads, writes, atomicity, version checks, retries, and persistence with the model. Fix mismatches or justify equivalent primitives.
- Use observed monitor coverage to select missing application tests; model coverage measures different evidence.
- Keep direct boundary, concurrency, and crash tests. Test encoded record limits and valid logical operations exceeding one record, including after history grows.
- A passing observation validates the delivered history against the monitor, not missing events, unobserved schedules, or assumptions.
- Options: [Python helpers](https://github.com/crowecawcaw/snappea/blob/main/python/src/snappea/__init__.py), [strict observation](https://github.com/crowecawcaw/snappea/blob/main/docs/observe.md#strict-observation).
# P syntax and commands

## Small syntax reference

Two fresh additions, one sequential client; no retries or failures. This checks actual results and completion, not just input validity.

```p
event eAdd: (client: Client, id: int, units: int);
event eReply: (id: int, total: int);
event eRequested: (id: int, units: int);
event eFinished;

// Contract: each fresh request adds its units; replies expose the stored total.
// Expectations come from client inputs, not Service's chosen result.
spec Contract observes eRequested, eReply, eFinished {
  var requests: map[int, int];
  var replied: set[int];
  var expected: int;
  start hot state Waiting {
    on eRequested do (p: (id: int, units: int)) {
      assert !(p.id in requests), "this example uses fresh request IDs";
      requests[p.id] = p.units;
    }
    on eReply do (p: (id: int, total: int)) {
      assert p.id in requests;
      assert !(p.id in replied);
      expected = expected + requests[p.id];
      assert p.total == expected, "reply disagrees with requested additions";
      replied += (p.id);
    }
    on eFinished do {
      assert sizeof(replied) == 2, "driver must complete both requests";
      goto Complete;
    }
  }
  state Complete { }
}

machine Service {
  var total: int;
  start state Serving {
    on eAdd do (p: (client: Client, id: int, units: int)) {
      // This add is the assumed atomic primitive. A load/CAS algorithm needs
      // separate steps; see cas-retry rather than making its whole API atomic.
      total = total + p.units;
      send p.client, eReply, (id = p.id, total = total);
    }
  }
}

machine Client {
  start state Run {
    entry (s: Service) {
      var step: int;               // all block locals precede statements
      var units: int;
      while (step < 2) {
        units = choose(2) + 1;     // generated inputs: 1 or 2, fresh IDs 0/1
        announce eRequested, (id = step, units = units);
        send s, eAdd, (client = this, id = step, units = units);
        receive { case eReply: (reply: (id: int, total: int)) {
          assert reply.id == step;
        } }
        step = step + 1;
      }
      announce eFinished;          // hot monitor catches an unfinished driver
    }
  }
}

machine Main {
  start state Init {
    entry {
      var s: Service;
      s = new Service();
      new Client(s);
    }
  }
}

// Select the spec explicitly. The set contains machines, never the spec itself.
test tcRequest [main=Main]: assert Contract in { Main, Service, Client };
```

- `int` is a type; `1` is an integer expression. A singleton tuple type is `(id: int)`; its expression uses a trailing comma, `(id = 1,)`. `choose(n)` explores integers from `0` through `n - 1`; `$` is a nondeterministic Boolean.
- Named composition: `module Model = { Main, Service, Client };` then `test tcRequest [main=Main]: assert Contract in Model;`. A machine name is not a module; specs go after `assert`, not in the machine set.
- Sequences: `s += (sizeof(s), value);` appends; `s -= (index);` removes. Sets: `items += (value);`. These are distinct operations.
- Maps use `map[keyType, valueType]`, membership (`k in m`), indexing (`m[k]`), and `keys(m)`. `foreach (item in collection)` iterates a set, sequence, or `keys(map)`; declare `item` as a local before the loop. Avoid using a machine field as the iterator.
- Put handler/function locals at the start of that block, before executable statements. P keywords such as `start`, `state`, and `event` are reserved; using one as a name causes a parse error, so rename it.
- `send target, event, payload` delivers to a machine; `receive { case event: (p: Type) { ... } }` waits; `announce event, payload` records a monitor-visible event. `send`, `receive`, and `new` are scheduling points; assignments do not yield.

## Checking

```sh
snappea check events.p monitor.p model.p tests.p --timeout 5s  # check, coverage, then mutations
snappea check 'tcRetry*' --timeout 5s
snappea check --no-mutation --timeout 5s # quick model iteration
snappea check mutation tcRetry --mutation-timeout 5s
```

- Check the top-level `summary` first: assertion failures, missing required coverage, and incomplete search are separate outcomes. Mutation limits alone do not fail the base check.
- Completion means the search finished within its budget; a timeout is incomplete evidence. Exhaustive completion covers only finite modeled bounds.
- Coverage shows which statements, branches, and handlers ran; it does not show that assertions enforce the contract. Mutation checking perturbs executed model code and reports changes surviving the test cases. Inspect survivors and add a discriminating scenario or independent assertion.
- For implementation histories, see [capture and observation](../guides/implementation.md). For a compact input-history generator, see [bounded history](../examples/bounded-history/bounded-history.p).
- Related examples: [reservation](../examples/reservation/Reservation.p), [durable retry](../examples/durable-retry/DurableRetry.p), [versioned publication](../examples/versioned-publication/Revalidated.p), and [CAS + generated retries](../examples/cas-retry/Balance.p).

